-- title:
--   Theorem 1(iii) (corrected: $\mathbb P^M\ne\mathbb P$) — investment and unhedgeable risk are the square roots of the two terms of $B(m,P,R)$
-- statement:
--   Throughout, the standing model of §3.1 holds (the published `HedgePSNV.Model.Standing`): $B,\tilde B$ independent Brownian motions with augmented filtrations $\mathcal F\supseteq\mathcal G$, the asset $dX_t=X_t(\mu_t\,dt+\sigma_t\,dB_t)$, the demand component $C$ with $A_T=C_T+\tilde\sigma\tilde B_T$, the two Markov hypotheses, and $\mathbb P^M$ with $d\mathbb P^M/d\mathbb P=Z_T$ (12); $V_0(P,R)=\mathbb E^M[H_T(P,R)]$, and $D=\{(P,R):P\ge c,\ R-bP\ge0\}$.
--
--   Suppose Assumptions 4–5 hold, $Z_T$ is not almost surely equal to $1$, and $\mathbb E[((C_T)^-)^2]<\infty$. Let $m,P,R\in\mathbb R$, let $V_t(P,R)=V_0(P,R)+\int_0^t\xi_s\,dX_s+\int_0^t\delta_s\,d\tilde B_s$ be a representation (18), and let $(\theta^*,\chi^*)$ be an optimal strategy of the hedging problem (14): admissible, with $\mathbb E[H_T(P,R)+\chi^*_T]=m$ and $\mathrm{Var}[H_T(P,R)+\chi^*_T]=B(m,P,R)$. Write $\theta^*=-\xi+\iota$ (20) and define
--   $$
--   \chi^{rm}_T=\int_0^T(-\xi_t)\,dX_t,\qquad \chi^{iv}_T=\int_0^T\iota_t\,dX_t,\qquad H^h_T=H_T(P,R)+\chi^{rm}_T\qquad(22),(23).
--   $$
--   Then $\chi^{rm}_T\in L^2(\mathbb P)$ and, with variances and covariances under $\mathbb P$,
--   1. $\mathrm{Var}(\chi^{iv}_T)+\mathrm{Cov}(\chi^{iv}_T,H^h_T)\ge0$ and $\mathrm{Var}(H^h_T)+\mathrm{Cov}(\chi^{iv}_T,H^h_T)\ge0$;
--   2. the **investment risk** satisfies
--   $$
--   \sqrt{\mathrm{Var}(\chi^{iv}_T)+\mathrm{Cov}(\chi^{iv}_T,H^h_T)}=\Big[\frac{[m-V_0(P,R)]^2}{Z^M_0-1}\Big]^{1/2}\qquad(24);
--   $$
--   3. the **unhedgeable risk** satisfies
--   $$
--   \sqrt{\mathrm{Var}(H^h_T)+\mathrm{Cov}(\chi^{iv}_T,H^h_T)}=\Big[\int_0^T\mathbb E\Big[\frac{Z_t}{Z^M_t}\delta_t^2(P,R)\Big]dt\Big]^{1/2}\qquad(25),
--   $$
--   with $\delta_t(P,R)=\tilde\sigma(P-s)\mathbb P^M(A_T\le R\mid\mathcal F_t)$ of (19).
--
--   The decomposition splits the minimal variance (21) of Theorem 1(ii) into the risk of the investment position and the risk the asset cannot hedge; Proposition 7 and Theorem 3 are read through it.
--
--   **Formalization Note** With $\chi\xi=\int\xi\,dX$, the Lean statement uses $\chi^{rm}_T=-\chi\xi_T$ and $\chi^{iv}_T=\chi^*_T+\chi\xi_T$. The right side of (25) is a lower Lebesgue integral in $[0,\infty]$ and its square root is the power $1/2$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: $\mathcal F_t$ and $\mathcal G_t$ are the filtrations generated by $(B_t,\tilde B_t)$ and $B_t$ (p.11) → the $\mathbb P$-augmented natural filtrations of $(B,\tilde B)$ and of $B$ (published `HedgePSNV.Model.Standing`); reason: martingale representation (Prop. EC.3) holds for the augmented Brownian filtration, and null-set modifications of an adapted process must stay adapted
--   - reading: $(X_t,\mu_t,\sigma_t)$ is Markovian under $\mathbb P$ and $(X_t,\sigma_t)$ is Markovian under $\mathbb P^M$ (pp.11–12) → the simple Markov property on $[0,T]$ with respect to $\mathcal F$ (`IsMarkovWrt`); reason: the paper does not define the term; this is the standard definition, relative to the filtration of footnote 4
--   - encoding: processes on $[0,T]$; stochastic integrals with almost-sure integrability → processes indexed by $\mathbb R_{\ge0}$, constrained for $t\le T$; the published relation `EthierKurtz.HasBrownianItoIntegral` plus a pathwise drift integral; reason: values after $T$ never enter; under the augmented filtration the integrand classes agree up to indistinguishability (published base convention)
--   - reading: $c$ the unit production cost, $s$ the unit salvage value, $X_0$ an asset price → $s<c$ and $x_0>0$ in the published parameter structure; reason: the newsvendor convention (p.7), implied by Assumption 3 wherever it is assumed; $X_t>0$ is used on p.ec28
--   - correction: Theorem 1 assumes only Assumptions 4–5 and asserts $Z^M_0=\mathbb E(Z_T^2)>1$ → the added hypothesis that $Z_T$ is not almost surely $1$ (i.e. $\mathbb P^M\ne\mathbb P$); reason: counterexample: $\mu\equiv0$, $\sigma\equiv1$, $C\equiv0$ satisfy Assumptions 4–5 with $Z\equiv1$, so $Z^M_0=1$, “$>1$” fails and the first term divides by zero; with $Z_T\ne1$, Jensen gives $\mathbb E Z_T^2>1$ (the published Theorem 1(i)–(ii) carry the same correction)
--   - reading: “the optimal solution” $\theta^*$ of (14) given by (20), with $\xi$ from (18) → every representation $(\xi,\delta)$ of (18) and every admissible $(\theta^*,\chi^*)$ meeting the mean constraint and attaining $B(m,P,R)$; reason: by the published Theorem 1(i) the optimal terminal gains are a.s. unique, so (22)–(25) are the same for every optimal strategy; this is the stronger reading
--   - encoding: $\chi^{rm}_T=\int_0^T(-\xi_t)dX_t$, $\chi^{iv}_T=\int_0^T\iota_tdX_t$ with $\iota=\theta^*+\xi$ → $\chi^{rm}_T=-\chi\xi_T$ and $\chi^{iv}_T=\chi^*_T+\chi\xi_T$, with $\chi\xi$ the gains of $\xi$; reason: linearity of the stochastic integral; the paper uses the same identity (26)
--   - encoding: $\mathrm{Var}$ and $\mathrm{Cov}$ of $\chi^{iv}_T$ and $H^h_T$, presupposed finite → Mathlib's `variance` and `covariance` under $\mathbb P$, with the added conclusion $\chi^{rm}_T\in L^2(\mathbb P)$; reason: the proof asserts $-\xi\in\mathcal A_X$ (p.ec43); the conclusion makes all second moments finite, so the real-valued variance and covariance are the paper's; it does not repair the proof's “$H_T$ is bounded”
--   - encoding: the square root of the second term of $B(m,P,R)$ (25) → `ENNReal.ofReal` of the left square root equals the lower Lebesgue integral to the power $1/2$; reason: the second term lives in $[0,\infty]$ as in the published Theorem 1(ii)
--   - encoding: the mean–variance problems (13)–(14) presuppose $\mathrm{Var}[H_T(P,R)]<\infty$ (p.9, p.ec23) → the hypothesis $\mathbb E[((C_T)^-)^2]<\infty$ (`NegPartL2`); reason: with the Gaussian term $\tilde\sigma\tilde B_T$ it is equivalent to $H_T(P,R)\in L^2(\mathbb P)$ for all $(P,R)$; it excludes only the case where problem (14) is undefined
--   - encoding: $B(m,P,R)$ the minimum of a variance over admissible hedges with mean $m$ (14) → the published `Bval`, an infimum in $[0,\infty]$ of the extended variance; reason: an empty feasible set gives $+\infty$, not a junk $0$
--   - encoding: an admissible strategy $\vartheta=\{\theta_t\}$ with $\chi_t(\vartheta)=\int_0^t\theta_sdX_s$ (EC.7.15) → a pair $(\theta,\chi)$ with $\chi$ an $\mathcal F$-adapted version of the gains on $[0,T]$; integrands progressively measurable; reason: the stochastic integral is fixed only up to null sets, so the strategy is carried with its gains (published `AX`)
-- source:
--   Wang–Yao–Zhang, How Does Risk Hedging Impact Operations? Insights from a Price-Setting Newsvendor Model, arXiv:2201.01026v4, Theorem 1(iii), (22)–(25), p.16 (PDF 16); proof p.ec43 (PDF 79)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_HedgePSNV_Hedging

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped NNReal ENNReal

namespace HedgePSNV.Supplement

open HedgePSNV

/-- Theorem 1(iii), arXiv:2201.01026v4, p.16 (corrected: `P^M ≠ P`, as the published Theorem
1(i)–(ii)): suppose Assumptions 4–5 hold and `Z_T` is not almost surely equal to `1`. Let
`(ξ, δ)` be the integrands of a representation (18) of `V(P,R)` and let `θ*` be the optimal
strategy of Theorem 1(i), with `θ* = −ξ + ι` (20). Define `χ^rm_T := ∫_0^T (−ξ_t) dX_t`,
`χ^iv_T := ∫_0^T ι_t dX_t` (22) and `H^h_T := H_T + χ^rm_T` (23). Then
`Var(χ^iv_T) + Cov(χ^iv_T, H^h_T) ≥ 0`, `Var(H^h_T) + Cov(χ^iv_T, H^h_T) ≥ 0`,
`√(Var(χ^iv_T) + Cov(χ^iv_T, H^h_T)) = [[m − V_0(P,R)]²/(Z^M_0 − 1)]^{1/2}` (24) and
`√(Var(H^h_T) + Cov(χ^iv_T, H^h_T)) = [∫_0^T E[(Z_t/Z^M_t) δ_t²(P,R)] dt]^{1/2}` (25).

Precisely: `χξ` is the gains process `∫ ξ dX`, so `χ^rm_T = −χξ_T`; `(θ, χ)` is any admissible
strategy that meets the mean constraint and attains `B(m,P,R)` (by the published Theorem 1(i)
its terminal gains are almost surely those of `θ*`), and `χ^iv_T = ∫ (θ + ξ) dX = χ_T + χξ_T`.
Variances and covariances are under `P`. The conclusion also states `χ^rm_T ∈ L²(P)`, which the
proof asserts (p.ec43, "−ξ ∈ A_X") and which makes the variances the paper's values.

**Formalization Note** With `µ ≡ 0` (`Z ≡ 1`) Assumptions 4–5 hold but `Z^M_0 = 1`, and (24)
divides by zero; the non-degeneracy `P^M ≠ P` is added exactly as in the published Theorem
1(i)–(ii). `NegPartL2` is the finite-variance presupposition of problem (14). The right side of
(25) is a lower Lebesgue integral in `ℝ≥0∞` and its square root is `^ (1/2)`. -/
theorem theorem_1_iii (p : Params) {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (M : Model p Ω)
    (hM : M.Standing) (hL2 : M.NegPartL2) (h4 : M.Assumption4) (h5 : M.Assumption5)
    (hne : ¬ (M.Z p.T =ᵐ[M.P] fun _ => (1 : ℝ))) (m P R : ℝ)
    (ξ δ χξ L : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (hξ : IsStronglyProgressive M.F ξ)
    (hδ : IsStronglyProgressive M.F δ) (hχξ : M.IsXGains ξ χξ) (hL : M.IsBtIntegral δ L)
    (hrepV : ∀ t : ℝ≥0, t ≤ p.T → M.V P R t =ᵐ[M.P] fun ω => M.V0 P R + χξ t ω + L t ω)
    (θ χ : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (hθχ : (θ, χ) ∈ M.AX)
    (hmean : ∫ ω, (M.H P R ω + χ p.T ω) ∂M.P = m)
    (hopt : evariance (fun ω => M.H P R ω + χ p.T ω) M.P = M.Bval m P R) :
    MemLp (fun ω => -χξ p.T ω) 2 M.P ∧
      0 ≤ variance (fun ω => χ p.T ω + χξ p.T ω) M.P +
        covariance (fun ω => χ p.T ω + χξ p.T ω) (fun ω => M.H P R ω - χξ p.T ω) M.P ∧
      0 ≤ variance (fun ω => M.H P R ω - χξ p.T ω) M.P +
        covariance (fun ω => χ p.T ω + χξ p.T ω) (fun ω => M.H P R ω - χξ p.T ω) M.P ∧
      Real.sqrt (variance (fun ω => χ p.T ω + χξ p.T ω) M.P +
        covariance (fun ω => χ p.T ω + χξ p.T ω) (fun ω => M.H P R ω - χξ p.T ω) M.P) =
        Real.sqrt ((m - M.V0 P R) ^ 2 / (M.ZM0 - 1)) ∧
      ENNReal.ofReal (Real.sqrt (variance (fun ω => M.H P R ω - χξ p.T ω) M.P +
        covariance (fun ω => χ p.T ω + χξ p.T ω) (fun ω => M.H P R ω - χξ p.T ω) M.P)) =
        (∫⁻ u in Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) (p.T : ℝ), ∫⁻ ω,
          ENNReal.ofReal (M.Z u.toNNReal ω / M.ZM u.toNNReal ω * M.delta P R u.toNNReal ω ^ 2)
            ∂M.P) ^ (1 / 2 : ℝ) := by sorry

end HedgePSNV.Supplement
